{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-102908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $r=3$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-102908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nr=3\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $z$-axis equals $3,$ while $\\theta $ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the circular cylinder shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/aa435c0ad4b4482c85a05af32afbbb7819b330a4292762ec66bad30a6ed31a44.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-102908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7afe0b53ce127f6857ce9a3081a59ed4378071997e3c2de9ff75d2bc3417a9b9"} {"question" "q-102908" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-102908" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular cylinder"] ["text" "A cone"] ["text" "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] ["text" "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"] ["text" "A sphere"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-102908" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A circular cylinder"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-102924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $z=r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-102924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{r}=1.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-102924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7afe0b53ce127f6857ce9a3081a59ed4378071997e3c2de9ff75d2bc3417a9b9"} {"question" "q-102924" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-102924" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular cylinder"] ["text" "A half-cone"] ["text" "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] ["text" "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"] ["text" "A sphere"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-102924" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A half-cone"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-104231" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =5$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-104231" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =5\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $5$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =5$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/a7e2f3f2ad24c353bc3dc63d82971b4888ccffc198479456beddd7fea185bd07.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-104231" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7afe0b53ce127f6857ce9a3081a59ed4378071997e3c2de9ff75d2bc3417a9b9"} {"question" "q-104231" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-104231" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular sector"] ["text" "A half-cone"] ["text" "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] ["text" "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"] ["text" "A sphere"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-104231" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-104240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What surface is defined by the equation ${z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-104240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the square of the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals half the square of the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}}=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}}}=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the tangent of this angle can be a positive or negative number, the $z$-coordinate can also be positive or negative.\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/7c8d0cefa5eb8da76f6cba59570670e4078c278c801aeb12344dce3183733149.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-104240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7afe0b53ce127f6857ce9a3081a59ed4378071997e3c2de9ff75d2bc3417a9b9"} {"question" "q-104240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-104240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circular cylinder"] ["text" "A cone"] ["text" "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis"] ["text" "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane"] ["text" "A sphere"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-104240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A cone"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-104244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/ee2764f5cf85f39107e458f347b048cc24227a55fae012b12f6cda4801cdfe78.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-104244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ z & =r\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nFinally, recall that $r\\ge 0.$ Since we wish to include both half-cones, we need to include $z=-r\\sqrt{3}$ as well. We can combine the two equations by squaring both sides.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is ${z}^{2}=3{r}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-104244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7afe0b53ce127f6857ce9a3081a59ed4378071997e3c2de9ff75d2bc3417a9b9"} {"question" "q-104244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-104244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=3\\pi r"] ["math" "z=3r"] ["math" "z=\\frac{r}{3\\pi }"] ["math" "{z}^{2}=3{r}^{2}"] ["math" "{z}^{2}=\\frac{{r}^{2}}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"} {"question" "q-104244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{z}^{2}=3{r}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4250e52d8e761665f152727909f41dfc121ae02747b5c3126665844bebb3f8a"}] "usage" {"q-102908" [] "q-102924" []} "basis" 1669 "retractions" [[17592186176101 :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $r = 3$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?"] [17592186176104 :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $z = r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186173269 17592186173270 17592186173271 17592186173272 17592186173273 17592186173287 17592186173288 17592186173289 17592186173290 17592186173291] "immutable_fields" [[17592186173268 :answer-field/correct] [17592186173268 :answer-field/id] [17592186173268 :answer-field/key] [17592186173268 :answer-field/type] [17592186173286 :answer-field/correct] [17592186173286 :answer-field/id] [17592186173286 :answer-field/key] [17592186173286 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
